    "q-74215": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74215",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14274391/assets/c9b2c979ee7356d3c8b638ad46cef3733d6c0db70965f1deec24413c5bf2136e.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to the given subset. But \n\n$$\n2\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case we have that the vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
