    "q-74217": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74217",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:1-x-y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:2x+3y-z=0\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
