    "q-34362": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34362",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x+3y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=2y+1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 0\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x+3y=0$ since $0+3\\cdot 0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x=2y+1$ since $0\\ne 2\\cdot 0+1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. There is only one vector in ${R}^{3}$ that satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 0,$ namely, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩.$ So, $C=\\{0\\}$, which is a special subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
