    "q-74256": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74256",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{t\\cdot ⟨2,-4,1⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:xzy=1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}>0\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the zero vector, $0.$ This can be verified by taking $t=0$, since \n\n$$\n0\\cdot ⟨2,-4,1⟩=⟨0,0,0⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $xyz=1$ since $0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}>0$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}=0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
