    "q-110761": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110761",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨2t,2+t⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=1\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have, for some $t$:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 2+t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2t=0 \\\\ 2+t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace.  It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $y=1$ since $0\\ne 1.$ So, $0$ is not in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
